# cezar environment variables — copy to `.env` and adjust. Every var is optional;
# the defaults give a zero-config local cockpit. cezar reads these from the process
# environment (it does not auto-load `.env` — export them, or use your shell/dotenv).

# ---- deployment mode --------------------------------------------------------
# Local (default) exposes host-only conveniences: "open in editor/Finder/terminal",
# worktree-path handoff, the launch bookmarklet. Set to 1 for VPS / hosted mode to
# hide those host-machine affordances (the cockpit is being viewed over the network).
# The Add project browser is always confined to CEZ_BROWSE_ROOT (default `~/`); set that
# root narrowly for a hosted cockpit if the remote viewer should not enumerate the whole home.
# CEZ_REMOTE=1

# ---- authentication (opt-in; unset is the zero-config single-user product) ---
# Which provider a sign-in is required against. Only the exact spellings `oidc` and
# `google` turn it on — anything else, including a typo, is treated as unset. Unset
# means zero I/O: no identity storage, no session middleware, no login route.
# Google is the generic OIDC implementation with a pinned issuer, not a second flow.
# CEZ_AUTH=oidc
#
# HOSTED MODE REQUIRES A CHOICE. cezar executes agents: POST /api/v1/workflows accepts a
# free-form `command` that a check step runs through `spawn('bash', ['-lc', …])`. A hosted
# deployment (CEZ_REMOTE=1, or a non-loopback --bind-host) with neither CEZ_AUTH nor the
# opt-out below REFUSES TO BOOT, naming the reason. This does not enforce auth — it enforces
# choosing, so nobody exposes a shell by forgetting a variable.
# Set this when your network or reverse proxy really is the perimeter; the server then boots
# and logs a warning naming the exposure on every start. `cezar server-install --platform
# ubuntu-vps` writes it into the systemd unit it generates, because that platform installs an
# nginx `auth_basic` vhost in front of the service.
# CEZ_ALLOW_UNAUTHENTICATED=1
#
# The deployment's own public origin, exact and absolute — the OIDC `redirect_uri` is derived
# from it, and it is the one thing a hosted deployment must NOT infer from a request header
# (trusting a forwarded Host for it is how open redirects happen). Required when CEZ_AUTH is set.
# CEZ_PUBLIC_URL=https://cezar.example.com
#
# Generic OIDC provider settings (discovery via <issuer>/.well-known/openid-configuration).
# With CEZ_AUTH=google the issuer is pinned and CEZ_OIDC_ISSUER is ignored.
# CEZ_OIDC_ISSUER=https://idp.example.com/realms/main
# CEZ_OIDC_CLIENT_ID=cezar
# CEZ_OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET=xxx
#
# Optional group -> role mapping. Defaults to none: an unrecognised group grants nothing, and
# membership is never inferred from a claim the operator did not map.
# CEZ_OIDC_GROUP_CLAIM=groups
# Only `admin` and `member` can be mapped — never `owner`, which is granted by onboarding.
# CEZ_OIDC_GROUP_ROLE_MAP=cezar-admins=admin,cezar-users=member
#
# WHO MAY CLAIM A FRESH DEPLOYMENT. The first user to name an organization becomes its owner,
# and an owner can run shell commands on this host — so arriving first must not be enough. With
# CEZ_AUTH set and no org yet, cezar mints a random BOOTSTRAP CODE at every start and prints it
# to its own log (journalctl -u cezar); the onboarding wizard asks for it, and refuses (403)
# without it. Nothing to set for the default. The code stops being printed, and stops granting
# anything, the moment the organization exists.
#
# Pin your own value instead of the generated one (useful for a config-managed install):
# CEZ_AUTH_BOOTSTRAP_TOKEN=some-long-random-string
#
# Or opt out and let whoever signs in first claim the deployment — say it deliberately, the way
# CEZ_ALLOW_UNAUTHENTICATED is said. Only the exact value `1`.
# CEZ_AUTH_BOOTSTRAP_OPEN=1

# ---- multi-org hosting (phase 6/7, D4/D10) — set on an ORG's process only ---
# CEZ_AUTH=supervisor names the phase-6+ shape: this process runs behind a supervisor that
# already authenticated the caller and forwards a signed, verified Principal — it does NOT
# terminate OIDC/Google itself (the supervisor's OWN process sets CEZ_AUTH=oidc/google instead).
# Never set this by hand outside a `server-install --platform hetzner`-provisioned host.
# CEZ_AUTH=supervisor
#
# Where THIS org's process reaches its supervisor to ask "who owns this project root" (D4's
# root->org registry, replacing the local IdentityStore an org process's own CEZ_HOME no longer
# has under D4's per-org uid boundary). Always loopback, same host as the supervisor — a bare
# port, not a URL. Required when CEZ_AUTH=supervisor.
# CEZ_SUPERVISOR_PORT=4000
#
# The per-org shared secret (OrgProcessRecord#supervisorSecret) this process authenticates its
# registry-client calls with, AND the one `forwarded-principal.ts` verifies the supervisor's
# signed request headers against. Minted at provisioning; delivered via a root-owned
# EnvironmentFile, never a plain Environment= line (readable by any local user). Required when
# CEZ_AUTH=supervisor.
# CEZ_SUPERVISOR_SECRET=xxx
#
# Make `serve`'s port a hard requirement instead of a preference: refuse to boot rather than
# silently drift to the next free port when it is busy (same as CLI `--port-strict`). Off by
# default — the npm-default `cezar serve` keeps drifting exactly as before. `server-install
# --platform hetzner` writes this into every ORG unit (never the supervisor's own), because an
# org's nginx proxy_pass is a specific loopback port baked in at provisioning time — a silent
# drift there is not a startup failure, it is another org's traffic routed into the wrong process.
# CEZ_PORT_STRICT=1

# ---- multi-org hosting — set on the SUPERVISOR's process only ---------------
# Domain= for the session cookie. UNSET (the default) is host-only, exactly as every release before
# phase 6 behaved — do not set it on a single-host install. Multi-org hosting needs it because the
# supervisor sets the cookie on login.<base> while each org answers on <org>.<base>: a host-only
# cookie is never sent to an org hostname, so nginx's auth_request subrequest sees no cookie and
# every org host 401s in a redirect loop. Must be the SHARED base domain with a leading dot, and it
# is a real widening — every subdomain of <base> now receives the cookie, so the base domain must
# be yours alone. `server-install --platform hetzner` writes it on the supervisor's unit ONLY: the
# supervisor is the only process that issues or clears a session cookie; an org's process is handed
# an already-verified principal and never touches one.
# CEZ_SESSION_COOKIE_DOMAIN=.cezar.example.com
#
# The supervisor's admin credential for its /internal/* provisioning surface (reading the org
# roster, registering and deprovisioning an org's process) — the calls `server-install --platform
# hetzner` makes on your behalf, which no org's per-org secret may make. Minted at provisioning
# into the supervisor's root-owned EnvironmentFile; you never type it. UNSET CLOSES the admin
# surface entirely rather than opening it: with no token there is no admin caller, and every
# admin-only route answers 403.
# CEZ_SUPERVISOR_ADMIN_TOKEN=xxx

# ---- dev launcher (scripts/dev.mjs) -----------------------------------------
# API port the cockpit server listens on and Vite proxies /api to. `npm run dev`
# probes for a free port and sets this automatically; set it only to pin a port.
# CEZ_API_PORT=4321

# ---- agent CLIs -------------------------------------------------------------
# Override the discovered path to each backend CLI (defaults: found on PATH).
# CEZ_CLAUDE_BIN=/usr/local/bin/claude
# CEZ_CODEX_BIN=/usr/local/bin/codex
# CEZ_OPENCODE_BIN=/usr/local/bin/opencode
# CEZ_PI_BIN=/usr/local/bin/pi

# The agents' OWN home variables (not cezar's) are honoured where the vendor documents one:
# setting one here moves that agent's DEFAULT account — the one cezar discovers. A SECOND login of
# the same CLI is deliberately NOT configured through the environment, because a process-wide
# variable cannot differ per project: add it in Settings → Agent accounts and pick it per project,
# and the two accounts can then be used side by side.
# CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR=~/.claude
# CODEX_HOME=~/.codex

# Claude denies tools that require approval instead of interrupting a headless run. Set to 1
# to opt back into Claude's interactive approval UI (`acceptEdits`).
# CEZ_APPROVAL_GATE=1

# Codex runs with full tool access by default. Set to 0 to opt into its network-blocked
# workspace-write sandbox.
# CEZ_CODEX_NETWORK=0

# ---- project folders --------------------------------------------------------
# Zero-config defaults for the two independent Add project folders. Saved values in Global
# Settings → Projects override these defaults. The browse root must already exist; missing
# checkout folders are created recursively when saved and when a clone starts.
# CEZ_BROWSE_ROOT=~/
# CEZ_PROJECTS_DIR=~/cezar/projects

# ---- local state and startup UI ---------------------------------------------
# Override cezar's global state directory (default: `~/.cezar`). Useful for isolated test or
# container installs; an empty value deliberately falls back to the default. This home holds
# `config.json` — the per-user workspace config (parallel cap, memory ceiling, project roots)
# and the project registry every repo cezar boots in registers itself into — so two cockpits
# started with different CEZ_HOME values serve disjoint sets of projects. Missing, corrupt or
# read-only: cezar falls back to defaults and keeps running.
# CEZ_HOME=/path/to/cezar-state

# Check and apply updates for tracked Open Mercato skill installations automatically. On by
# default; set to 0 to disable. A saved Skills setting overrides this environment default.
# CEZ_SKILLS_AUTO_UPDATE=0
# New Task defaults. Exact 0/1 only; workspace Settings overrides these seeds.
# CEZ_AUTONOMOUS_DEFAULT=0
# CEZ_WORKTREE_DEFAULT=1

# Dangerous escape hatch for any run executing in the repository root, including an
# explicit worktree opt-out, non-Git degradation, or a continuation whose worktree
# cannot be restored. Only the exact value 1 bypasses the repository-root lease.
# Concurrent agents may overwrite each other's files or Git state; isolated worktrees
# are unaffected. Off by default.
# CEZ_DISABLE_REPO_LOCK=1

# Opt into a launch-project-only cockpit. Only the exact value 1 enables this mode
# (`true`, `yes`, and every other value leave the default multi-project workspace on).
# While enabled, cezar exposes only the repo it was launched in and refuses project add,
# edit, checkout, browse, and remove operations. Registry rows are retained: unset the
# variable and restart to restore the full workspace without migration or data loss.
# CEZ_SINGLE_PROJECT=1

# Hide token counts or backend-reported cost independently throughout the browser
# cockpit. The legacy CEZ_HIDE_TOKEN_METRICS master switch hides both. Only the exact
# value 1 enables any presentation-only mode; telemetry and API payloads are preserved.
# Restart cezar after changing these flags.
# CEZ_HIDE_TOKEN_USAGE=1
# CEZ_HIDE_COST=1
# CEZ_HIDE_TOKEN_METRICS=1

# Skip the open-mercato/skills banner printed by `cezar serve`. Dismissing it in the cockpit
# also silences the terminal banner without requiring this variable.
# CEZ_NO_BANNER=1

# ---- follow-up inbox --------------------------------------------------------
# Off by default. Set to 1 to turn the global follow-up inbox back on: agents are then asked
# to leave follow-ups in `.ai/cezar/todos.json` when they finish, the Inbox view reappears in
# the cockpit, and the composer offers a per-task toggle. Left off, agents are never asked and
# the Inbox is hidden — the per-task handoff journal ("Notes") is a separate feature and always
# runs. Existing todos.json entries are kept either way, so this is safe to flip back and forth.
# CEZ_FOLLOWUPS=1
# The browser suite exercises the default-off route. To also exercise the enabled inbox in a
# fresh shared test environment, run: `CEZ_FOLLOWUPS=1 npm run test:e2e -- --force`.

# ---- GitHub automations -----------------------------------------------------
# Off by default. Set to 1 to turn GitHub automations on: the Automations view appears in the
# cockpit, its endpoints answer, and cezar polls GitHub on each enabled automation's interval
# while it is running. Only the exact value 1 enables it (`true`, `yes`, and every other value
# leave it off), and it is read at boot — set it and restart cezar. Left off, nothing polls
# GitHub and no task is ever launched from it. Automation definitions, receipts and their
# frozen high-watermarks are kept either way, so this is safe to flip back and forth.
# CEZ_AUTOMATIONS=1
# The browser suite exercises the default-off route. To also exercise enabled automations in a
# fresh shared test environment, run: `CEZ_AUTOMATIONS=1 npm run test:e2e -- --force`.

# ---- task auto-naming ---------------------------------------------------------
# Short LLM-generated task titles (spec 2026-07-17-task-auto-naming). On by default.
# CEZ_TITLE_UPDATES=0 turns off the live per-turn title refresh (the Settings → Agents
# toggle, when set, wins over this env default). CEZ_AUTONAME=0 is the kill-switch for
# ALL LLM naming — creation-time and live — leaving only the heuristic titles. Under
# CEZ_DRY_RUN=1 naming is off by default (mock titles would clobber honest heuristics);
# CEZ_AUTONAME=1 forces it there — used by the test suite.
# CEZ_TITLE_UPDATES=0
# CEZ_AUTONAME=0

# ---- review gate -------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional diff-first review gate (#489). OFF by default: a successful run with
# changes settles straight to `done`, leaving the diff in the worktree. Set
# CEZ_REVIEW_GATE=1 to make a changed non-autonomous run park at `review` for a
# human to Accept / Send back / open a Draft PR. Only the exact string "1"
# enables it ("true"/"yes" are treated as off). The Settings → Agents toggle,
# when set, wins over this env default. Autonomous runs ALWAYS skip the gate.
# CEZ_REVIEW_GATE=1

# ---- periodic autosave -------------------------------------------------------
# Off by default. Set to 1 to re-enable the janitor-style "cezar autosave" commit every 90 s
# in each task worktree (spec 006). Left off, a task branch carries only the agent's own
# commits — plus the turn-end and pre-PR flushes, which always run so the branch still ends
# holding the finished state and the diff/review/PR views stay complete. Those flushes commit
# as `cezar autosave (turn end|run finalize|pre-PR)`; only `(periodic)` is this flag's timer.
# CEZ_AUTOSAVE=1

# ---- GitHub -----------------------------------------------------------------
# Used for the GitHub tab / PR actions. The `gh` CLI's own auth is preferred; set
# this only if you drive git/gh through a token instead.
# GITHUB_TOKEN=ghp_xxx

# ---- knowledge base (central-hub scaffold, inert until CEZ_KB=1) ------------
# Off by default. Only the exact value 1 turns on a knowledge base indexing the project's
# own committable roots plus any explicitly configured mounts: no scan, no index, no served
# content, no nav item, and a byte-identical agent system prompt while unset.
# CEZ_KB=1
# Optional upgrade over the default BM25-plus-identifier-pin search. Off by default; needs
# CEZ_KB=1 to do anything. The embeddings blob lives entirely under the gitignored
# knowledge-index/ cache directory, never in the committable knowledge/ content roots.
# CEZ_KB_EMBEDDINGS=1
# CEZ_KB_EMBEDDINGS_API_KEY=sk-...
# Override the embeddings endpoint/model; both are defaulted, so neither is required.
# CEZ_KB_EMBEDDINGS_URL=https://api.openai.com/v1/embeddings
# CEZ_KB_EMBEDDINGS_MODEL=text-embedding-3-small

# ---- external source connectors (inert until CEZ_SOURCES=1) -----------------
# Off by default. Mirrors a remote source (Notion in phase 1) to read-only Markdown files
# the knowledge base can index, once CEZ_KB=1 also registers the mirror as a mount.
# CEZ_SOURCES=1
# The Notion credential: environment only, never written to disk and never a token field in
# the UI. Falls back to NOTION_TOKEN then NOTION_API_KEY if unset.
# CEZ_NOTION_TOKEN=secret_...
# Override the Notion API base — used by the test suite to point at a local stub. Defaults
# to https://api.notion.com; no reason to set this outside tests.
# CEZ_NOTION_API_BASE=https://api.notion.com

# ---- cross-project views (inert until CEZ_WORKSPACE_VIEWS=1) ----------------
# Off by default. CEZ_WORKSPACE_VIEWS=1 turns on a read-only "all projects" runs board.
# Reports false under CEZ_SINGLE_PROJECT=1 regardless of its own value, because a
# single-project cockpit has nothing to aggregate.
# CEZ_WORKSPACE_VIEWS=1

# ---- the notes inbox (inert until CEZ_NOTES=1) ------------------------------
# Off by default, and exactly "1" — any other spelling leaves the feature absent.
# CEZ_NOTES=1 turns on a workspace-scoped capture inbox at /notes: write one free-form
# note, and on your click an agent pass reads the whole board and proposes tasks across
# the projects the note implies. Approving a proposal starts a run in THAT project which
# writes a spec and stops; implementing it is a separate, later click. Nothing is
# analysed and nothing is started without a click — see
# .ai/specs/2026-08-14-note-to-spec-pipeline.md.
# Notes live in ~/.cezar/notes.json, never inside a project. Reports false under
# CEZ_SINGLE_PROJECT=1: a cross-project inbox has nothing to be cross.
# CEZ_NOTES=1

# ---- notification transports (inert until CEZ_NOTIFY=1) ---------------------
# Off by default. The ONLY switch; every other spelling, including true/yes, leaves the
# feature absent: no registry, no observer, no timer, no route behind the nav, no file
# created under ~/.cezar/notifications/.
# CEZ_NOTIFY=1
# Container bootstrap: setting BOTH of these with no config file synthesizes exactly one
# enabled webhook transport with the default event matrix. Unset means neither yields
# anything, and CEZ_NOTIFY_WEBHOOK_TOKEN matches the secret-redaction pattern, so it is
# auto-scrubbed from every persisted transcript.
# CEZ_NOTIFY_WEBHOOK_URL=https://ntfy.sh/my-topic
# CEZ_NOTIFY_WEBHOOK_TOKEN=xxx
# Per-transport credential referenced by a transport's own `auth.envVar` (the documented
# default spelling is CEZ_NOTIFY_<ID>_TOKEN). CEZ_NOTIFY_TOKEN is the specific name the
# companion chat-repo spec pins for the single Loki Messages `loki` transport row.
# CEZ_NOTIFY_<ID>_TOKEN=xxx
# CEZ_NOTIFY_TOKEN=lok_...
# Override the discovered deep-link base a notification body points at (otherwise: the
# server-install domain if one is recorded, else the loopback address).
# CEZ_COCKPIT_URL=https://cezar.example.com

# ---- cockpit bundle (build time) --------------------------------------------
# Where the cockpit's JS should look for the API. Empty (the default) means "the origin that
# served this page", and that is right in BOTH normal cases: the bundled cockpit is served by the
# CLI itself, and `npm run dev` proxies `/api` (including the `/api/v1/ws` upgrade) from the Vite
# dev server to the local service — so a relative path already reaches localhost either way, and
# setting this in development would only bypass the proxy.
# Set it only when the bundle is deployed apart from the service it talks to. A deployment that
# must be configured without a rebuild can instead put `<meta name="cez-api-base" content="…">`
# in the served HTML, which wins over this.
# VITE_CEZ_API_BASE=http://localhost:4321

# ---- testing / internal -----------------------------------------------------
# Run agents against a mock CLI (no real model calls) — used by the test suite.
# CEZ_DRY_RUN=1

# Lock model selection to the defaults in native Claude/Codex/OpenCode settings.
# Existing `.ai/cezar/config.json` model presets are preserved but ignored while locked.
# `"modelsLocked": true` in global `~/.cezar/config.json` or a repository's
# `.ai/cezar/config.json` enables the same policy.
# Exact `1` also delegates provider authentication/enablement to the native
# agents: Cezar skips its credential probes and provider-disable preferences,
# while Claude/Codex/OpenCode runner selection remains available.
# CEZ_AGENT_MODELS_LOCKED=1
# CEZ_MOCK_ARGS_FILE=/tmp/cez-mock-args.json
# How far out the mock CLI's `mock:limit` reply puts its usage-limit reset (seconds, default 60),
# so a CEZ_DRY_RUN=1 session can exercise auto-resume without waiting for a real window.
# CEZ_MOCK_LIMIT_RESET_SECONDS=60
# The following are set by cezar for child agent processes; you should not set them:
#   CEZ_TASK_ID, CEZ_HANDOFF_FILE, CEZ_TODOS_FILE
#   CEZ_KB_ROOTS, CEZ_KB_WRITE_FILE — added to that env only while CEZ_KB=1; with the flag
#   unset neither key is present at all, so the zero-config agent env is unchanged.
